{
  // Source metadata for the build. The Astro adapter (@astrojs/cloudflare via
  // @cloudflare/vite-plugin) injects `main` and `assets` and writes the deploy
  // config to dist/server/wrangler.json. Do not set `main` or `assets` here.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "sakola-kembara-web",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  // The API Worker (cf/admin), reached through an internal SERVICE BINDING, and only through its
  // named entrypoint `PublicApi` (src/api/public/entrypoint.ts there). Two things go through it:
  // the contact form (POST /api/public/contact, which exists ONLY on that entrypoint) and every
  // read (GET /api/public/*: /tim, /laporan, /gabung-siswa/docs and the blog). A binding needs no
  // public path to the API and no shared secret, and because the API Worker serves no
  // /api/public/* route on its public face, nobody on the internet can reach the read endpoints
  // with cache-busting query strings and spend the daily D1 read budget
  // (docs/cloudflare/public-api.md).
  // `service` is a PLACEHOLDER: the deployed API Worker's real name is chosen at deploy time and
  // must not be an account-specific value in git. `sakem-admin` is the `name` in
  // cf/admin/wrangler.jsonc (a test keeps the two equal): change both together if the deployed
  // name differs. Without `entrypoint` the binding would reach the public face, where neither the
  // reads nor the contact route exist: every API call would be a 404. A test pins the entrypoint.
  // Locally the binding is not used: CONTENT_API_URL and CONTACT_API_URL point at
  // scripts/fixture-api.mjs (dev.vars.example), and they win over the binding when set.
  "services": [{ "binding": "API", "service": "sakem-admin", "entrypoint": "PublicApi" }],

  // STAGING (SAKEM-084, docs/cloudflare/staging-runbook.md): the same code as another Worker on `*.workers.dev`
  // in the organisation account. This file is read by the Vite plugin of the Astro adapter, which picks the
  // environment from `CLOUDFLARE_ENV` at BUILD time (`pnpm build:staging`) and writes the flattened result to
  // dist/server/wrangler.json, with the staging name in it: whatever is deployed from that build is the staging
  // Worker, whatever `--env` says. `pnpm deploy:staging:dry` builds and dry-runs it. A build WITHOUT
  // CLOUDFLARE_ENV is the production Worker (name above).
  //   - `services` is not inherited, so it is written out again, and it names the STAGING admin Worker
  //     (`<name>-<environment>` of cf/admin/wrangler.jsonc: wrangler's rule for a binding that targets an
  //     environment). A test (tests/staging-config.test.ts) keeps the two names equal.
  //   - Per-deploy public values (TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY, ADMIN_URL, UPLOADS_BASE_URL) are not in git: they are passed
  //     with `--var` (cf/scripts/staging-deploy-command.mjs prints the command from an untracked file). NEVER set
  //     CONTENT_API_URL or CONTACT_API_URL, here or there: they would move the reads and the contact form off the
  //     binding. Staging has no origin behind its hostname (no WordPress), so a pass-through path such as /wp-login.php or
  //     /wp-content/* would fetch the Worker's own address again (Cloudflare's loop limit is 16 invocations); the deploy command passes
  //     LEGACY_ORIGIN_URL as a `*.invalid` host, which never resolves, so it fails at once (docs/cloudflare/free-plan-audit.md).
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "sakem-web-staging",
      "workers_dev": true,
      "preview_urls": false,
      "services": [{ "binding": "API", "service": "sakem-admin-staging", "entrypoint": "PublicApi" }]
    }
  }

  // Intentionally NOT set here (no resource ids, hostnames or secrets in git):
  //
  // - "routes": the production Worker is attached to the zone with a ROUTE
  //   (pattern covering the whole site), not a custom domain, so that
  //   fetch(request) inside the Worker reaches the legacy origin behind the
  //   same hostname. Add it at deploy time. See docs/cloudflare/web-skeleton.md.
  // - "vars": LEGACY_ORIGIN_URL (unset in production: plain fetch);
  //   TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY (the PUBLIC site key of the Turnstile widget on /kontak, set per deploy;
  //   the matching secret lives on the API Worker only); optional: TEAM_CACHE_FRESH_SECONDS,
  //   CONTENT_CACHE_FRESH_SECONDS. NEVER set CONTENT_API_URL or CONTACT_API_URL in production: they
  //   are for local runs and CI (a URL wins over the binding), and set in production they would move
  //   the reads or the contact form off the binding onto public HTTP. Locally the vars come from an
  //   untracked .dev.vars; copy dev.vars.example (it carries Cloudflare's always-pass TEST site key).
  // - UPLOADS_BASE_URL (a "vars" entry too): the public origin of the R2 bucket that holds the files the
  //   admin uploads, e.g. `https://files.<your-domain>`, without a trailing slash. The API keeps the
  //   paths root-relative (`/uploads/...`); this site puts the origin in front when it renders team
  //   photos, report and document files, blog covers and the images and links inside an article.
  //   Empty or unset (local, CI, today) changes nothing. Set it with the deploy (`wrangler deploy
  //   --var UPLOADS_BASE_URL:https://...` or the deploy config), never in git. Steps for the owner:
  //   docs/cloudflare/uploads.md.
  // - PAGE_CACHE_SECONDS (a "vars" entry too, optional, public): seconds a rendered page that depends only on the
  //   API's data (/blog, an article, /tim, /laporan, /gabung-siswa/docs, /sitemap.xml) is kept in the isolate's memory
  //   and answered from there, with no render. Unset or 0: every request renders (what the site did before SAKEM-091).
  //   At most 120; 30 is the value to start with. src/lib/page-cache.ts, docs/cloudflare/web-skeleton.md.
  // - WEB_ANALYTICS_BEACON_ID (a "vars" entry too, optional, PUBLIC): the Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon id (what Cloudflare calls
  //   the site token: exactly 32 hexadecimal characters; it is in every page's HTML, so it is not a secret, and the name has no
  //   TOKEN in it so the deploy-command printer keeps refusing secret names). Set with the deploy (`--var
  //   WEB_ANALYTICS_BEACON_ID:<32 hex characters>`), never in git. It reaches the pages the WORKER renders (/blog, an article,
  //   /tim, /laporan, /gabung-siswa/docs, /kontak, the 404 page). The prerendered pages (/, /donasi, /gabung-siswa, /program/<id>)
  //   are static files and take the same name from the shell environment of the BUILD: `WEB_ANALYTICS_BEACON_ID=<...> pnpm build`
  //   (never from .dev.vars). Set it in both places, from one value. Unset or invalid: no beacon script and no tag at all, which
  //   is what local runs, tests and staging do. src/lib/web-analytics.ts, docs/cloudflare/web-skeleton.md ("Web Analytics").
  // - PORTAL_ENABLED (a "vars" entry too, optional, PUBLIC; SAKEM-103, Phase A of the cutover): whether the student portal is live.
  //   Exactly `true` puts the navbar's "Masuk" link and its session script on the page; unset, `false` or anything else (the Worker
  //   says so once, without the value) leaves both out and the navbar offers "Daftar Sekarang" (`/daftar`) instead. The default
  //   is OFF and Phase A runs with it unset. Like the beacon it has two sources: `--var PORTAL_ENABLED:true` at deploy reaches the
  //   pages the WORKER renders, and `PORTAL_ENABLED=true pnpm build` in the shell of the BUILD reaches the prerendered ones (never
  //   .dev.vars). Set both, from one decision, when Phase B brings the portal. src/lib/portal-enabled.ts, docs/cloudflare/web-skeleton.md
  //   ("The student portal switch").
  // - LEGACY_ORIGIN_URL, in production: leave it unset. The Worker's route sits in front of the proxied WordPress record, so the
  //   pass-through of `/wp-admin`, `/wp-login.php`, `/wp-content`, `/wp-includes`, `/wp-json`, `/xmlrpc.php` and `/login` is a plain
  //   fetch of the same URL, which reaches WordPress behind the hostname (src/lib/legacy-paths.ts). Staging passes a `*.invalid` host.
  // - "ratelimits": the rate-limit binding of the contact form, binding name CONTACT_LIMITER:
  //     "ratelimits": [{ "name": "CONTACT_LIMITER", "namespace_id": "<a positive integer you choose>",
  //                      "simple": { "limit": 5, "period": 60 } }]
  //   The code uses `env.CONTACT_LIMITER` when it exists and an in-memory limiter (one isolate, a
  //   development fallback) when it does not, so the form works either way. The numbers are the
  //   legacy limit (5 per minute per visitor address); the API Worker enforces the same behind it.
}
